# G038 — Port Scanner

*v2. See `port-scanner.md` for prose: systematic boundary exploration,
three-state result (open/closed/filtered), well-known ports as
service registry, concurrent probing, scan comparison as temporal
diff.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Infrastructure Audit

[infra-audit
    IN ORDER TO
        scan two hosts concurrently, compare against last scan
        and emit security signals only when the port surface changed

    concurrent [
        droplet: @droplet-scan <- [scan {host: "144.126.251.126",
                                          ports: @common-ports}]
    |
        local:   @local-scan   <- [scan {host: "127.0.0.1",
                                          ports: @common-ports}]
    ]
    join

    @droplet-diff <- [scan[compare
                             {before: @last-droplet,
                              after:  @droplet-scan}]]
    @local-diff   <- [scan[compare
                             {before: @last-local,
                              after:  @local-scan}]]

    @droplet-diff
        where newly-opened count is greater than zero
            -> #unexpected-ports {host: "droplet",
                                   ports: @droplet-diff:newly-opened}
        where expected-services (5432, 8080) are missing
            -> #service-down {host: "droplet",
                               missing: @droplet-diff:newly-closed}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[scan
    new              {host, ports}
    probe            {host, port, timeout-ms}
                                             ;; -> open | closed | filtered
    well-known       {port}                   ;; -> service name | none
    compare          {before, after}           ;; -> {newly-opened, newly-closed}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: three-state result `where` silence means filtered

[scan[probe {host: @h, port: @p, timeout-ms: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        distinguish between "the port said no" and "the network ate the packet"
        because a firewall silently dropping is neither open nor closed

    attempt TCP connect to (@h, @p)
        where replies with SYN-ACK   -> open
        where replies with RST       -> closed
        where timeout elapses @t     -> filtered (no reply at all)
]

G013's structural validation was binary. G038 adds *unknown* as a
first-class state — the same shape InnateScript's `@reference`
needs: resolves, explicitly refuses, or hangs. The resolver's
timeout distinguishes refusal from silence.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: concurrent probe `where` each is independent

[scan[new {host: @h, ports: @ps}]
    @ps
        mapped concurrently via [scan[probe {host: @h, port: @p}]]
        joined into a scan-report
        ordered by port ascending
]

The concurrent/join shape is G066's fan-out; the result is an
aggregate not a list of disconnected outcomes. Each probe is pure
and independent — no shared state means no coordination cost.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @before <- [scan[new {host: "127.0.0.1",
                           ports: [22, 80, 443, 5432, 8080]}]]
    ;; say 22, 5432 open; 80, 443 closed; 8080 filtered

    [shell[start-service {name: "nginx"}]]   ;; opens 80
    @after <- [scan[new {host: "127.0.0.1",
                          ports: [22, 80, 443, 5432, 8080]}]]

    -> [scan[compare {before: @before, after: @after}]]
    ;; -> {newly-opened: [80], newly-closed: [], unchanged: [22, 5432, 443, 8080]}
]
